---
import Page from '../../layouts/Page.astro';
import ReviewWindow from '../../components/ReviewWindow.astro';
import { nav } from '../../data/site';

// The four building blocks, each pointing to its section on this page
const parts = nav[0].items.slice(1).map((p) => ({ ...p, anchor: '#' + p.href.split('/').pop() }));
---
<Page
  title="Product"
  description="ArborSpace on one page: the problem, the ontology, the rules, the review queue and the deployment."
  eyebrow="Product"
  heading="Expert knowledge the system can apply and people can review."
  lead="ArborSpace is not a chatbot over your documents, nor a better search, nor a data platform for dashboards. It captures what your experts know in an ontology."
  cta={false}
>
  <section class="alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">The building blocks</p>
        <h2>Four parts, one memory.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="cards">
        {parts.map((p) => (
          <a class="card" href={p.anchor}>
            <h3>{p.label}</h3>
            <p>{p.description}</p>
            <span class="more">Read below ↓</span>
          </a>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap doc-layout">
      <nav class="doc-toc" aria-label="Contents">
        <p class="eyebrow">Contents</p>
        <ol>
          <li><a href="#problem">The problem</a></li>
          <li><a href="#what-it-is">What ArborSpace is</a></li>
          <li><a href="#ontology">Ontology</a></li>
          <li><a href="#rules">Rules</a></li>
          <li><a href="#review-queue">Review queue</a></li>
          <li><a href="#deployment">Deployment</a></li>
          <li><a href="#pilot">The pilot</a></li>
        </ol>
      </nav>
      <article class="prose">
        <h2 id="problem">The problem</h2>
        <p>
          Lenders, investors, traders and their advisers decide on assets they do not operate.
          The data comes from someone else: a seller’s maintenance plan, an operator’s records, a
          data room. What makes a judgement sound are the checks your senior analysts make by
          reflex, such as comparing a planned outage with the last one, or following a turbine
          through to the debt it supports.
        </p>
        <p>
          Those checks live in their heads and in old reports. Each engagement starts over,
          depends on who is available, and the conclusions rarely show where they come from. The
          tools at hand do not close the gap: business applications each hold one slice of the
          asset, data platforms store everything as tables whose meaning is rebuilt by hand, and
          dashboards show numbers, not reasons.
        </p>

        <h2 id="what-it-is">What ArborSpace is</h2>
        <p>
          ArborSpace is the memory of the firm. It captures what your experts know in a form the
          system can apply and your teams can review: the objects of your business, the links
          between them, and the checks your analysts make on them. Every conclusion traces back
          to its source before a person signs it off.
        </p>

        <h2 id="ontology">Ontology</h2>
        <p>
          The ontology is an operational layer for the organisation. It sits on top of the data
          already integrated and holds the objects, their properties and the links between them,
          along with the logic that applies to them. It is built from the firm’s own vocabulary:
          asset classes, engagements, stakeholders and how they relate.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>A domain model, not a blank page.</strong> ArborSpace ships with a first model
          of the domain (energy assets, due diligence engagements) that the firm adapts rather
          than builds.
        </p>
        <p><a href="/product/ontology">More on the ontology →</a></p>

        <h2 id="rules">Rules</h2>
        <p>
          The unit of value is the rule: a piece of expert knowledge written down explicitly,
          which the system applies and anyone can review. Rules run against the data, and a
          similarity engine matches each case with the firm’s previous ones.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Readable rules.</strong> Rules are declarative and inspectable: every
          conclusion traces back to a named rule and an explicit path of relationships.
        </p>
        <p class="callout">
          <strong>Example:</strong> a vibration above 8.2&nbsp;mm/s on a class 7F gas turbine
          generates a finding. That finding is linked to the asset, the site, the engagement and
          the lender concerned, along with the rule that produced it.
        </p>
        <p><a href="/product/rules">More on the rules →</a></p>

        <h2 id="review-queue">Review queue</h2>
        <p>
          People decide. Every conclusion arrives as a finding in a review queue. The expert
          approves or rejects it, and that decision feeds the memory.
        </p>
        <div class="doc-figure"><ReviewWindow /></div>
        <p><a href="/product/review-queue">More on the review queue →</a></p>

        <h2 id="deployment">Deployment</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>On your premises.</strong> ArborSpace is deployed in the client’s environment,
          on site or in its own cloud, with code the client can audit.
        </p>
        <p><a href="/product/deployment">More on deployment →</a></p>

        <h2 id="pilot">The pilot</h2>
        <p>
          Six weeks on a narrow scope of your firm: fifteen rules, applied to your own
          engagements. <a href="/resources/pilot">How the pilot works →</a>
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
</Page>
